WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:04.800
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.100
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.400
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.640
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.460
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:19.440
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.600
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:25.660
Hi, everybody.

9
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.560
Hello from our closets.

10
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:30.880
Still here.

11
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.080
Still in our closets.

12
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.780
Still surrounded by Josh's many, many hats.

13
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.440
So many baseball hats.

14
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.140
So many.

15
00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:40.880
Does he have a favorite?

16
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.100
Can we get him on the pod to ask him to give us a breakdown of these hats?

17
00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.820
Are they significant emotionally in some way?

18
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.060
Do they represent a team he loves?

19
00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:51.380
I mean, no.

20
00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.360
I think Josh is all about the fit.

21
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.860
And it looks like he loves the Patagonia kind of trucker-looking hat.

22
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.140
It does with the adjustable band thing, the little plastic snappies.

23
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.480
Josh, I hope you are excited that we shared.

24
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.300
Listen, there's other things in the closet.

25
00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.840
Right over my shoulder, I notice all the time that there's the, what's the stuff they spray in their shoes, Jenna?

26
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:15.840
Febreze?

27
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:16.620
No.

28
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:18.900
All dudes do this.

29
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.720
They spray that stuff in their shoes.

30
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:21.560
Odor eaters?

31
00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:22.760
Yes, yes.

32
00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:23.320
Anyway.

33
00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:24.780
Oh, now, Josh, you're really happy.

34
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.420
I've shared with everyone that you spray your shoes.

35
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:38.800
Well, because we need to talk about this longer, I am really curious about the very bright red pair of shoes over your shoulder in the shoe cubby.

36
00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:39.920
Whose are those?

37
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.040
Oh, those are my red converse.

38
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.160
Okay, so here's the thing.

39
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:47.740
Yes, this is Josh's closet, but I occupy most of the shoe space.

40
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:49.940
I have lots of shoes.

41
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.320
You have overflow that goes into his closet.

42
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.920
Yeah, he's real happy about it.

43
00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.320
Well, guys, it's what you're here for, a breakdown of Angela's closet.

44
00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.780
Next week, we'll do my closet.

45
00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.160
But this week, we are talking about chair model.

46
00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.400
It's so good.

47
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.220
Season 4, Episode 14, written by BJ Novak, directed by Jeffrey Blitz.

48
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.040
With a duo like that, you know it's going to be good.

49
00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:20.640
I love the simple idea that Michael has a thing for the model in a chair catalog.

50
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.480
I just love how simple and perfect that is.

51
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:24.140
Yes.

52
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:25.780
Let me give you a summary.

53
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:26.620
Do it.

54
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:35.340
Michael is unable to pick a new chair for his office after he becomes infatuated with a woman modeling office chairs in a catalog.

55
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.960
He decides he's ready to rejoin the dating pool.

56
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.980
He's inspired by his love of the chair model.

57
00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:50.740
Meanwhile, the Dunder Mifflin parking spaces are being used by a construction crew doing work for W.B.

58
00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:51.420
Jones.

59
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.320
And the staff of Dunder Mifflin has to park in a satellite lot.

60
00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:03.880
Kevin and Andy join forces to confront the other bosses of the Scranton Business Park to reclaim their places in the main lot.

61
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:05.660
Simple storylines.

62
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.000
Absolute perfection.

63
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.180
Also, absolutely true.

64
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:09.820
Yeah.

65
00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:14.640
You know, I had to park in a satellite lot when I had my admin job.

66
00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:22.220
If you were an executive, you could park in the parking lot of the building, this high rise in downtown Los Angeles.

67
00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:22.880
Yeah.

68
00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.980
I did not get a parking space.

69
00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.740
I had to park in a satellite lot that was about a half a mile away.

70
00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.980
And it wasn't a big deal, except for on days that it rained.

71
00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:34.140
Well, here's the thing.

72
00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.860
You know where you rank in a company by where you park.

73
00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:37.740
Oh, yeah.

74
00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:39.880
Well, Michael has a spot right up front.

75
00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:40.920
He's not concerned.

76
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.860
Well, later he's going to try to imagine what it would be like if he didn't have that spot.

77
00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:46.700
Mm-hmm.

78
00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:51.900
But it takes two employees angrily confronting him for him to get there.

79
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:54.400
What are our fast facts, Jenna?

80
00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.440
Fast fact number one.

81
00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:01.460
The original title of this episode was Michael Dating.

82
00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:02.460
Hmm.

83
00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:09.900
But they realized that they would have to release the name of the episode before Dinner Party had aired.

84
00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:11.920
Oh, and they didn't want that.

85
00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.320
They didn't want to ruin the surprise about Jan and Michael's breakup, right?

86
00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:16.220
Yes.

87
00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.260
So they changed the name of the episode to Parking.

88
00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:25.440
But then they felt, well, while it's a big part of the episode, it's not the main storyline of the episode.

89
00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.880
So they eventually settled on Chair Model, which I personally love.

90
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.080
I mean, when you see that, aren't you intrigued?

91
00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.380
I'm so intrigued.

92
00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.420
What is this episode going to be about?

93
00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:38.260
Chair Model.

94
00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.080
I'm more intrigued than Parking.

95
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:40.660
I'll say that.

96
00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.260
They picked the right one.

97
00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:43.040
All right.

98
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:44.120
Fast fact number two.

99
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:49.240
I think we need to do a little breakdown of the Scranton Business Park five families.

100
00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.400
I mean, it's like Goodfellas.

101
00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:52.440
It is.

102
00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:53.900
All right.

103
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.140
Here are the bosses of the five families.

104
00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:00.220
Michael Scott, of course, Bob Vance, Vance Refrigeration.

105
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.160
Then there is W.B.

106
00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.800
Jones from W.B.

107
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:04.740
Jones Heating and Air.

108
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:12.240
He was played by Barry Sigismondi, Bill Kress of Kress, Tool, and Die, who was played by Terrence Beeser.

109
00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:20.760
And finally, Paul Faust of Disaster Kits Limited, who was played by Paul FauSt.

110
00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.400
This is an amazing story.

111
00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:30.380
And it all starts when an actual man named Paul Faust came to visit the set of The Office.

112
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:31.520
Yes.

113
00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.820
So I have known Paul Faust for years.

114
00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.600
We used to be related by marriage.

115
00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.520
And I reached out to him.

116
00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:39.000
And he is just such a hoot.

117
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:43.540
And he sent in all of these fantastic audio clips about his day on set.

118
00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:45.040
They are amazing.

119
00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:50.280
And in this first one, he explains exactly how he got the role on the show.

120
00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:52.340
Sam, you got to play this clip.

121
00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.900
Hey, office ladies and office fans.

122
00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:57.660
This is Paul Faust, a.k.a.

123
00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:58.560
Cool Guy Paul.

124
00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:00.520
I want to tell you how I got a part of the show.

125
00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:01.840
So I was out in L.A.

126
00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:07.540
on business and decided to visit the set because my cousins are part of the office.

127
00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.180
And when I was out there, I met the writers.

128
00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:13.840
And I kind of told them one of my companies and what I did, which was a company that sells disaster kits.

129
00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.180
And Greg had said, this is my cousin.

130
00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:17.460
He's a little nuts.

131
00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:18.200
Tell him what you do.

132
00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:19.580
I told him about the company.

133
00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:27.360
And when I walked out of the room, unbeknownst to me, BJ Novak said, that guy's got to be a character on the show.

134
00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.020
So he wrote a part based on me.

135
00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:34.700
And the day before they shot the scene, they hadn't found an actor to play the part.

136
00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:38.780
So I got a call asking me if I wanted to audition to play the part.

137
00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:45.320
And long story short, I got the part, got to fly out and be part of the awesome show and play myself.

138
00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:48.220
So that's how I got to be on the show.

139
00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:50.580
It's really a story only can happen in Hollywood.

140
00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:52.560
I mean, that's bonkers.

141
00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:53.580
I know.

142
00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.200
He's literally playing himself.

143
00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:59.840
But what's bonkers to me about it was that they did hold auditions.

144
00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.080
They held auditions for this role.

145
00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:06.940
And the night before, they just had not found someone who could play Paul FauSt.

146
00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:08.860
So they're like, how about Paul Faust?

147
00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:11.240
Paul really sells disaster kits.

148
00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:12.300
That's real.

149
00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:13.840
That is his company.

150
00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:14.760
Oh, yeah.

151
00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:17.420
And Angela, he talked about that in this next clip.

152
00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:22.820
I think one of the most unique things about me being in the office was that I actually played myself.

153
00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.040
When I went out to L.A.

154
00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:28.840
and I was visiting and I went to the set, I talked about one of the businesses I run,

155
00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:32.780
which is a company called 1-800-PREPARE, 1-800-PREPARE.com,

156
00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:36.240
where we actually sell disaster kits and preparedness kits.

157
00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:37.480
So I talked about it.

158
00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:40.400
And that's what DJ had picked up on.

159
00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:45.120
And so he wrote a part called about Cool Guy Paul, the owner of Disaster Kits Limited.

160
00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.140
And, yes, I still own that company.

161
00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:49.720
We still sell disaster kits.

162
00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:57.200
So I think I'm one of the few people who played himself on TV with his real name in a real business that he runs,

163
00:07:57.820 --> 00:07:58.800
which was kind of cool.

164
00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.300
So we asked Paul, how did he prepare, you know, to have these scenes?

165
00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:05.340
He's not an actor.

166
00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:11.420
He's not used to learning lines or where to sit, you know, how to navigate a set and all that kind of stuff.

167
00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:12.580
And here's what he had to say.

168
00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.220
So I got this part, let's say, on a Thursday.

169
00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:18.700
And I had we were shooting Friday morning.

170
00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:22.720
So I had about a good 10 hours to learn my five or six lines.

171
00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:25.100
So I'm studying, studying on the plane.

172
00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:25.780
I'm reading them.

173
00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:26.680
I didn't sleep.

174
00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:27.360
I studied them.

175
00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.840
So we go there and this whole experience happens.

176
00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:30.660
And you're nervous.

177
00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:31.960
And there's a lot of people around.

178
00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.180
And I had 10 hours to learn a bunch of lines.

179
00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:36.180
So I was able to do it.

180
00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.320
And we knocked it out.

181
00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:38.700
And I got it done.

182
00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:39.980
And I was like, great, I finished.

183
00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:42.480
And then all of a sudden, somebody came in.

184
00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:44.620
And they threw these new scripts down.

185
00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:46.320
They said, OK, here's the alternate ending.

186
00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.420
Let's take a five-minute break, learn our lines, and come back at you.

187
00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.300
And I'm like, wait, wait, wait, what a minute?

188
00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:52.240
Five minutes, what?

189
00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:54.340
I had 10 hours to learn my lines.

190
00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:56.580
Five minutes, I'm not learning new lines.

191
00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:02.180
So I raced out the hallway, had to study, learn all the lines, learn when I came in and whatever.

192
00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:05.660
I wound up doing it, knocking it out, getting it done right.

193
00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:11.200
And again, life experience with amazing people who were just so supportive.

194
00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:14.820
And I was glad that I was able to be a tiny part of this amazing family.

195
00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:17.940
Well, Angela, I found an interview with Paul.

196
00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:22.240
And he said that he landed in LA at 1.30 in the morning.

197
00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:24.980
The airline lost his luggage.

198
00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.220
So he had no luggage.

199
00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:31.360
He got to his hotel and went to sleep about 3.15 in the morning.

200
00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:33.120
And he had to be up at 5 a.m.

201
00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:33.740
for work.

202
00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:37.500
So after all of that, I'm kind of amazed.

203
00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:38.940
I would have been loopy.

204
00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:40.760
I wouldn't have been able to learn new lines.

205
00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:41.600
No.

206
00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:45.440
And I think of Paul, just like he's had no sleep.

207
00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.400
He's probably in the same underwear because they lost his bag.

208
00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:54.240
And he, you know, and now he's like, he thought he reached the finish line.

209
00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:56.780
And then they hand him this whole new task.

210
00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:01.280
Well, Paul sent a few more clips about his experience just being on set.

211
00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:02.160
They're so delightful.

212
00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:04.340
We're going to sprinkle those in throughout the episode.

213
00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:05.240
All right.

214
00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.080
Fast fact number three, Angela.

215
00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:07.820
Mm-hmm.

216
00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:12.140
I'm titling this, Am I the Inspiration for This Episode?

217
00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:14.240
I'm sorry, what?

218
00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:15.340
Were you a chair model?

219
00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:16.520
No.

220
00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:23.640
But in season one of the show, I wanted a new chair at reception.

221
00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:25.980
And I had to lobby for it.

222
00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:29.900
So I'm sure I'm not the inspiration for this episode.

223
00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:34.620
But in season one, they gave me a chair at reception without arMs.

224
00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:36.360
It was an armless chair.

225
00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:36.980
Mm-hmm.

226
00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:39.520
And I really, really wanted a chair with arMs.

227
00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:45.860
And so I attempted to try to sort of move the chairs or find a chair maybe that hadn't been

228
00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:47.060
claimed or used.

229
00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:48.420
It wasn't working.

230
00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:53.480
And big shout out to our set dresser, Steve Rothstein, because he is one of the guys who

231
00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:56.140
headed up the team that picked out all our furniture on the office.

232
00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:58.420
And he got me a chair with arMs.

233
00:10:58.760 --> 00:10:59.540
Oh, lady.

234
00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:01.160
And I was so excited.

235
00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:03.480
Well, I have a chair story for you.

236
00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.260
I've been holding on to this.

237
00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:07.400
I'm going to go ahead and tell you now.

238
00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.300
The chair I'm sitting in right now, ready?

239
00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:11.500
I'm going to pan down.

240
00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:12.840
Do you see this chair?

241
00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:13.960
I do.

242
00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:16.140
It's a blue chair with mesh.

243
00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:17.840
Do you recognize it?

244
00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:20.840
I don't recognize it, but it has no arMs.

245
00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:23.160
Phil Shea gave me this chair.

246
00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:27.200
It was a leftover chair from Workbus.

247
00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:29.100
Oh, my gosh.

248
00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:32.280
You've been sitting in a Workbus chair this whole time?

249
00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:34.920
For a year in my closet.

250
00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:38.440
I have been sitting in my Workbus chair.

251
00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:43.160
And I was going to save it for Workbus, but we're having all this chair talk.

252
00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:44.540
I couldn't hold it in anymore.

253
00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:51.280
Well, I just have to say this is why our show is so great, because getting a chair is

254
00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.420
a very relatable big deal moment.

255
00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:59.020
When you are in an office job or any job, your chair, you sit in it all day, and they

256
00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:01.780
created this amazing, wonderful storyline out of it.

257
00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:03.160
Yes.

258
00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:06.340
And thank you, Phil Shea, because I had gotten a desk.

259
00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:09.600
You know, I had in the corner of my guest room a little desk where I would sit and do

260
00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.960
work, and I didn't have a proper desk chair.

261
00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:15.660
I was using one of the kitchen chairs, and I was sort of talking about it on set.

262
00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:18.740
And Phil Shea was like, Ange, I have some extra chairs from Workbus.

263
00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:19.660
Do you want one?

264
00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:21.000
I was like, yes.

265
00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:22.740
This is making me happy.

266
00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:23.600
I know.

267
00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:25.160
We all have a chair story.

268
00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:29.620
And I have been sitting in it for a year with you and haven't told you because I was going

269
00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:30.980
to try to work it in a Workbus.

270
00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:32.280
But there you go.

271
00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:33.560
Let's take a break.

272
00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:35.800
And when we get back, we'll get into it.

273
00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:36.560
All right.

274
00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:47.630
We are back.

275
00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:50.850
We open on a Jim and Pam talking head.

276
00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:53.990
They are standing outside of the Dunder Mifflin office.

277
00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:59.410
And they are explaining that due to some renovations in the building, all of their parking spots

278
00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:04.490
are being taken up, and they have to park in a satellite lot and walk to work.

279
00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:06.890
They're being really positive about it, though.

280
00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:07.610
Yeah.

281
00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:11.210
They saw a junkyard dog eating, you know, some of a chicken.

282
00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:11.890
Yeah.

283
00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:17.110
Eating the bones of a rotisserie chicken, Angela, which was based on a real story.

284
00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:18.630
Yeah, it was.

285
00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:25.790
There was a junkyard next to our real office building, and there was a junkyard dog who

286
00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:30.130
they would throw, like, old rotisserie chicken bones to.

287
00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:32.970
We've talked about this junkyard and the dog.

288
00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:36.290
We got very invested in this dog's life that lived over there.

289
00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:37.050
We did.

290
00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:38.950
And it made it into an episode.

291
00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:42.970
So then we have Oscar and Andy, who are less positive.

292
00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:47.890
And then there's Kevin, who is just downright angry.

293
00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:52.030
Kevin is going to have a breakdown over the satellite parking lot.

294
00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:52.590
Yeah.

295
00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:57.490
Well, Andy says he's so bummed because he lost a penny out of his penny loafers.

296
00:13:57.810 --> 00:13:58.230
Yeah.

297
00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:01.470
Jenna, I don't understand penny loafers, so I looked them up.

298
00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:02.190
Oh.

299
00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:03.690
You could have asked me.

300
00:14:03.830 --> 00:14:07.090
I wore penny loafers all through high school as part of my school uniform.

301
00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:10.010
Okay, well, why did you put a penny in your penny loafer?

302
00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:13.450
I don't know, because there was a slot there for it.

303
00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:14.870
Okay, well, I'll tell you.

304
00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:16.050
I'll tell you why.

305
00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:17.790
Oh, my God.

306
00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:20.490
Well, you have put me in my place.

307
00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:23.150
I clearly don't know about penny loafers.

308
00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:24.750
You told me I could have asked you.

309
00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:26.790
Well, you're not answering my question.

310
00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:29.410
Why a penny in your loafers?

311
00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:35.730
So, according to the internets, back in the 1930s, the pay phone in the phone booth

312
00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:37.650
cost two cents.

313
00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:43.270
The new loafer design allowed just enough space for a penny in each shoe,

314
00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:45.870
equaling the cost of an emergency phone call.

315
00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:48.950
Thus, the penny and the loafer were united.

316
00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:51.990
But only men could make emergency phone calls.

317
00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:52.410
Oh!

318
00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:56.950
If you were a woman, so sorry.

319
00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:00.410
You don't even get a pocket to put your pennies in.

320
00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:01.570
Good luck, lady.

321
00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:03.470
You're just going to die on the street.

322
00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:06.530
But for the men, we have pockets.

323
00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:08.690
You can hide money in your shoes.

324
00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:12.050
Where else can we make life easy for you, gentlemen?

325
00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:16.670
That took a turn, but point taken.

326
00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:22.030
So now the episode starts, and Jenna, it would have started with a deleted scene

327
00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:25.690
with Michael checking out of the Shroop FarMs.

328
00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:27.330
What?

329
00:15:27.710 --> 00:15:28.090
Yeah.

330
00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:30.310
Oh, because he's been staying with Dwight?

331
00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:31.090
Yes.

332
00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:35.910
So there was an alternate beginning where Michael is leaving the farm, and he's like,

333
00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:36.830
it's moving day.

334
00:15:37.090 --> 00:15:38.910
You know, I've been here about a month.

335
00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:43.670
I let Jan have the condo, and I'm moving out of Shroop FarMs.

336
00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:47.730
And as he's leaving, Dwight goes hold up and hands him a bill.

337
00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:52.890
And Michael's like, wait, you were charging me this whole time?

338
00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:54.590
And Dwight was like, yeah.

339
00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:57.650
And then they have this funny back and forth about it.

340
00:15:57.830 --> 00:15:58.810
Oh, my gosh.

341
00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:04.210
My mind is blown by the backstory that Michael lived with Dwight for a month.

342
00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:10.390
And it does sort of show why maybe now he's looking at the chair model like he is,

343
00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:12.710
because he's a month past his breakup.

344
00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:17.170
And I don't know, maybe he's ready for something new.

345
00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:18.170
Yeah.

346
00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:21.550
Oh, can we see this deleted scene somewhere?

347
00:16:22.090 --> 00:16:24.070
Yes, it's on the DVDs.

348
00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:25.010
Amazing.

349
00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:28.850
So the scene they went with is this one in his office.

350
00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:35.370
He's looking through the catalog and really just gazing at this lady in this chair.

351
00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:38.390
Well, we had some fan mail about it.

352
00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:43.230
Carly D., Jenna A., Mariah K., and Charlotte S.

353
00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:45.870
all wrote in to ask who played the chair

354
00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:47.470
model in the picture.

355
00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:50.590
The chair model was played by April Eden.

356
00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:54.030
She would go on to play Trish, a.k.a.

357
00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:57.570
Miss Pawnee, in two episodes of Parks and Recreation.

358
00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:00.030
Well, I have a little background catch.

359
00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:06.490
At a minute, nine seconds, there's a wonderful shot of the chair model, but the magazine is

360
00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:08.730
sitting on top of some message slips.

361
00:17:09.110 --> 00:17:09.570
Oh.

362
00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:12.870
And I zoomed in on the message slip.

363
00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:17.730
The message that was written down said, Andrea, oof or ooter, guys?

364
00:17:17.870 --> 00:17:18.330
I don't know.

365
00:17:18.430 --> 00:17:19.190
I couldn't make it out.

366
00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:24.210
From Dunder, Louisville, called and asked Michael to please call as soon as he returns.

367
00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:29.510
And the number was 307-555-4438.

368
00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:31.570
So, I called it.

369
00:17:32.290 --> 00:17:33.790
Well, that's a fake number.

370
00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:38.030
Well, yeah, it rang fast busy, but I wasn't sure, so I called it.

371
00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:44.090
No, you know that thing where in movies, they always start phone numbers with 555 because

372
00:17:44.090 --> 00:17:46.350
nobody has that as a prefix?

373
00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:51.770
Jenna, I know that, but we've had a phone number and it ended up being a sex hotline.

374
00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.330
So, I decided to do my due diligence.

375
00:17:54.330 --> 00:18:01.770
It is a phony baloney number, but the area code 307 is a Wyoming area code, but there is

376
00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:03.030
no Louisville in Wyoming.

377
00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:04.430
There you have it.

378
00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:05.150
Boom!

379
00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:11.030
Everything you needed to know about this message slip next to the catalog.

380
00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:13.430
Angela, I admire you.

381
00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:15.250
I do.

382
00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:17.310
I really love that detail.

383
00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:24.170
Pam just needs Michael to pick a chair so that she can get his chair.

384
00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:30.470
But he is now obsessed with the chair model and Pam is very sad.

385
00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:35.350
But then Michael has a talking head where he says he really likes being single because

386
00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:38.130
he can start off each day with a sense of possibility.

387
00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:42.030
And this was my favorite part when this talking head takes a turn.

388
00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:49.110
He says, and each day becomes more desperate and desperate situations yield the quickest

389
00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:49.830
results.

390
00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:53.310
Which is kind of true.

391
00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:54.550
It is true.

392
00:18:55.170 --> 00:19:01.050
There was an alt talking head here that was pretty amazing.

393
00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:06.990
Jenna, you know the nursery rhyme where it's like, Pam and Jim sitting in a tree.

394
00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:08.750
K-I-S-S-I-N-G.

395
00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:09.450
You know that one?

396
00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:09.950
Yes.

397
00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:15.650
So, he's sort of used this nursery rhyme as a template for how to find your perfect match.

398
00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:17.950
And Sam, can you play it?

399
00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:22.670
Love, marriage, baby carriage.

400
00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:28.550
Those have been my goals ever since I heard that song.

401
00:19:29.890 --> 00:19:31.490
Jenna and I had love.

402
00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:33.890
We did not have marriage.

403
00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:40.710
We did have a baby carriage, which I got her for bringing groceries home after she got a DUI.

404
00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:47.930
So messed up.

405
00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:56.350
And I have to say, Jenna, in this scene with you, when he says, remember after my dinner party when I said that I was swearing off women?

406
00:19:56.530 --> 00:20:02.390
The way you said, I definitely remember your dinner party was so good.

407
00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:04.510
You just crushed it.

408
00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:07.490
I love doing that scene with Steve.

409
00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:12.030
I remembered when I was watching it, how much we were laughing.

410
00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:13.810
It was just so fun.

411
00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:15.290
That was a good memory.

412
00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:20.950
It's a very subtle, like, reminder to the audience what just came before this.

413
00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:21.710
Yeah.

414
00:20:22.090 --> 00:20:22.650
Okay.

415
00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:27.430
Well, now, Jenna, we have a creed-talking head that is phenomenal.

416
00:20:27.890 --> 00:20:29.310
It is phenomenal.

417
00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:37.330
He says, when Pam gets Michael's old chair, he gets Pam's old chair, which will bring him to two chairs.

418
00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:39.150
Only one more to go.

419
00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:41.850
Why does he need so many chairs?

420
00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:44.170
You're not the only person who's wondering.

421
00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:45.750
We got a lot of mail about it.

422
00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:47.650
So I texted Creed.

423
00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:48.870
Oh, no.

424
00:20:49.050 --> 00:20:53.150
And he had a lot of answers for why he needed three chairs, and I'm going to share them all.

425
00:20:53.330 --> 00:20:53.830
Wait, wait.

426
00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:57.630
Is this sort of like Creed, the actor giving Creed the character backstory?

427
00:20:58.050 --> 00:20:58.430
Yes.

428
00:20:58.430 --> 00:20:59.330
Oh, I love it.

429
00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:00.230
All right.

430
00:21:00.470 --> 00:21:01.570
Option number one.

431
00:21:01.850 --> 00:21:03.730
He was growing weed in the ceiling.

432
00:21:03.990 --> 00:21:07.110
It was the same place where Angela threw her cat.

433
00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:14.430
Now, he had access through a crawl space outside, but he needed to get in there at night by his desk.

434
00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:21.550
So after finally getting his three chairs and climbing into the ceiling, they collapsed one night, and he was stranded in the ceiling.

435
00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:24.350
He wants you to know that's a whole thing that could have happened.

436
00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:27.930
Creed, what the hell are you talking about?

437
00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:30.890
You're going to stack three office chairs?

438
00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:33.370
I think all three of them had wheels.

439
00:21:33.970 --> 00:21:34.190
Yeah.

440
00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:36.150
Well, here's option number two.

441
00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:44.670
He said Creed stole many things from the office, and he needed the extra chairs for a set because he was selling it to a competitive paper company.

442
00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:46.090
That, I believe.

443
00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:46.630
Yes.

444
00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:49.310
I believe Creed had many side hustles.

445
00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:51.230
Well, here's his final answer.

446
00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:58.450
After perfecting his cartwheel, he wanted to use the three chairs as part of an obstacle course to really challenge himself.

447
00:21:58.450 --> 00:22:00.570
Well, that could have been very true.

448
00:22:01.650 --> 00:22:03.730
Oh, Creed, you're so fun.

449
00:22:03.890 --> 00:22:06.450
Thank you for humoring us time and time again.

450
00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:17.470
Well, Michael tells everyone in the bullpen, get out your catalogs, open them to page 85, and take a look at the woman in the catalog.

451
00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:21.110
Yeah, use that as a template for who you're going to set me up with.

452
00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:22.990
Jenna, this was an announcement.

453
00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:27.650
He came out of his office to address his employees.

454
00:22:27.930 --> 00:22:35.790
Could you imagine working with a boss that made a huge announcement about how you should go about setting him up?

455
00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:36.810
No.

456
00:22:37.070 --> 00:22:41.830
Later, he says if you don't provide him a name, by the end of the hour, you're going to be fired.

457
00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:43.290
We'll get there.

458
00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:44.650
But it's crazy.

459
00:22:44.650 --> 00:22:46.890
So now, here's what I noticed.

460
00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:52.170
We all do get out these catalogs, and we start opening up to page 85.

461
00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:54.950
And I remember these catalogs, Angela.

462
00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:55.930
Do you remember them?

463
00:22:56.410 --> 00:22:56.850
Yes.

464
00:22:57.090 --> 00:22:59.310
So we got mail about this, too.

465
00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:09.610
Mariah, Charlotte, and Christina all wanted to know, did the props department have to make an entire magazine just for these scenes with the catalog?

466
00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:10.630
No.

467
00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:15.170
So what they did was they had like an actual, I don't remember what it was, Angela.

468
00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:15.410
Do you?

469
00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:18.630
I think it was Staples or Office Depot or something.

470
00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:21.470
And they glued a new cover on it.

471
00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:21.830
Yes.

472
00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:23.970
So it was like a Dunder Mifflin cover.

473
00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:24.470
Yeah.

474
00:23:24.590 --> 00:23:33.990
And then on the inside, on page 85, they glued this two pages so that when we opened it up, we could show those two pages.

475
00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:36.930
But the rest of it was an actual office catalog.

476
00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:37.790
That's right.

477
00:23:37.790 --> 00:23:38.097
Right.

478
00:23:38.097 --> 00:23:43.370
And they just had to print basically the cover and then the two insert pages that we would all turn to.

479
00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:43.870
Right.

480
00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:51.150
Jenna, you might notice there's not a lot of reaction shots of me in this because my belly was so big.

481
00:23:51.310 --> 00:23:52.590
I'm peak preggers here.

482
00:23:52.730 --> 00:23:57.310
That is why I'm not anyone that walked in, you know, like from the satellite parking lot.

483
00:23:58.030 --> 00:24:05.610
So, Jenna, I don't know if you remember, but this week on our set, Chris Hastings, our NBC set photographer, was there and he took a picture of me at my desk.

484
00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:08.230
I'm completely out of character and I'm pointing at my belly.

485
00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:09.450
Look, Jenna, here it is.

486
00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:10.530
Oh, my God.

487
00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:11.330
You're huge.

488
00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:12.190
I know.

489
00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:14.150
Anyway, I'll put that in stories.

490
00:24:14.410 --> 00:24:16.590
But, yeah, it was getting really hard to hide my belly.

491
00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:21.710
Well, there were enough shots of you, Angela, and I noticed that they were all very close up.

492
00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:23.310
They're like shoulders and up.

493
00:24:23.550 --> 00:24:30.250
But it's enough for me to notice that you were wearing a blouse with tiny keys on it.

494
00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:32.370
Little, like, skeleton keys.

495
00:24:32.570 --> 00:24:33.570
What was it?

496
00:24:33.910 --> 00:24:35.170
Lady, look at this picture.

497
00:24:35.290 --> 00:24:36.070
It's not a blouse.

498
00:24:36.170 --> 00:24:37.070
It's a freaking dress.

499
00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:39.330
It was a whole dress of it.

500
00:24:39.550 --> 00:24:41.270
It was a turtleneck dress.

501
00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:43.570
I mean, it looked like a muumuu.

502
00:24:43.570 --> 00:24:49.890
It was very comfortable, but we were starting to struggle with what could fit me over my belly

503
00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:53.350
and look like business attire of someone who was not pregnant.

504
00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:54.090
Yeah.

505
00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:55.890
I have a question for you.

506
00:24:56.190 --> 00:24:56.790
What is it?

507
00:24:57.070 --> 00:25:02.590
At 3 minutes 35 seconds, you'll notice that Kevin and Michael are sitting on the sofa by front reception.

508
00:25:03.050 --> 00:25:04.930
Michael's wanting Kevin to set him up.

509
00:25:05.130 --> 00:25:06.950
He's asking him about Stacy.

510
00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:12.790
Do you know why they're sitting on the sofa and why Michael isn't in accounting talking to Kevin?

511
00:25:12.790 --> 00:25:17.930
Well, I know that Kevin's feet are in a foot bath.

512
00:25:18.590 --> 00:25:19.990
Is that why, though?

513
00:25:20.450 --> 00:25:21.110
Yes, Jenna.

514
00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:26.770
There's a whole deleted scene where you're trying to answer the phone, but Kevin's foot bath, it's so loud.

515
00:25:26.770 --> 00:25:30.170
It sounds like a ginormous hot tub, but it's a little foot bath.

516
00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:34.670
So for the majority of this episode, Kevin is sitting there next to front reception.

517
00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:44.090
Yes, he is soaking his feet in his foot bath that he bought himself for the Christmas party episode, that very first episode.

518
00:25:44.410 --> 00:25:46.410
And it's such a wonderful callback.

519
00:25:46.550 --> 00:25:50.490
But since they cut that scene at reception, you can't even really see it.

520
00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:54.630
I tried to even find a time code where you could see the foot bath and you can't.

521
00:25:54.710 --> 00:25:55.430
You can't.

522
00:25:55.430 --> 00:25:59.870
You can see, just like Kevin's pants are hiked up, but you're like, why are they hiked up?

523
00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:00.190
Yeah.

524
00:26:00.270 --> 00:26:02.250
Why am I seeing Kevin's bare ankles?

525
00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:06.350
But it's because they're resting in a foot bath, and that's in the deleted scenes.

526
00:26:06.790 --> 00:26:08.830
Well, now Michael's going to walk around the office.

527
00:26:09.230 --> 00:26:15.770
He's going to try to make pleas to various people in the office to see if they have any single friends that they can set him up with,

528
00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:22.170
which leads to a very funny runner with Phyllis about her friend Sandy.

529
00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:24.850
Phyllis is so good in this scene.

530
00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:25.750
So good.

531
00:26:25.910 --> 00:26:29.290
And the way Steve fires off his questions about Sandy.

532
00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:33.150
I mean, Phyllis says, look, she's gorgeous with a feisty personality.

533
00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:36.710
And Michael's like, so wait, jolly or sassy?

534
00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:37.570
Right.

535
00:26:37.690 --> 00:26:39.490
And Phyllis is like, no.

536
00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:42.070
And he's like, well, what does she do for a living?

537
00:26:42.650 --> 00:26:46.410
And Phyllis is like, she's a professional softball player.

538
00:26:46.870 --> 00:26:49.910
And Michael's like, catcher or infield?

539
00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:54.030
And she's like, I don't know.

540
00:26:54.750 --> 00:26:59.430
Oh, and then he asks her if she could fit in a rowboat, in a standard-sized rowboat.

541
00:26:59.590 --> 00:27:01.350
And Phyllis gets so angry with him.

542
00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:02.770
She's like, fine, no, Michael.

543
00:27:03.230 --> 00:27:05.150
No, she can't fit in a rowboat.

544
00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:07.970
Oh, it's really amazing writing.

545
00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:10.490
Michael really loses it now, Angela.

546
00:27:10.490 --> 00:27:16.150
And he gives this impassioned speech where he says he wants to play ball with his kids

547
00:27:16.150 --> 00:27:17.590
before he's too old.

548
00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:20.650
But first, he needs to get laid.

549
00:27:21.050 --> 00:27:23.170
And that means he needs to be in love.

550
00:27:23.690 --> 00:27:26.090
Oh, that kind of made me love Michael for saying that.

551
00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:31.110
He just wants the picket fence and the kids and the two-car garage.

552
00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:35.150
And he just, Michael is one of these people.

553
00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:37.310
And anyone out there that's had children,

554
00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:40.350
you know how you have one of your kids who just wants a family?

555
00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:40.990
Yes.

556
00:27:41.550 --> 00:27:44.150
And Michael was that kid since he was a little boy.

557
00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:45.530
All he wanted was a family.

558
00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:49.170
I mean, if you, and not to be a downer,

559
00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:53.230
but I don't think Michael had a great family experience as a child,

560
00:27:53.230 --> 00:27:56.050
and he's still longing for it as an adult.

561
00:27:56.370 --> 00:27:57.170
Of course.

562
00:27:57.450 --> 00:27:59.710
That's like part of his whole driving force.

563
00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:00.170
Yeah.

564
00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:03.650
Is to find family, whether it be at work or with a partner.

565
00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:04.750
He wants family.

566
00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:05.790
Yes.

567
00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:09.210
Well, this is now, Angela, where Michael says,

568
00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:13.550
everybody, I want you to give me the name of someone

569
00:28:13.550 --> 00:28:17.390
that he can go on a date with within the hour or they're fired.

570
00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:20.130
And Dwight is going to hand out index cards.

571
00:28:20.530 --> 00:28:22.990
That he's already written prompts for.

572
00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:24.230
Did you see that?

573
00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:24.850
Yeah.

574
00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:25.590
He's ready.

575
00:28:25.810 --> 00:28:26.610
He's ready.

576
00:28:27.290 --> 00:28:29.190
Well, listen, let's take a break.

577
00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:31.970
And when we come back, we will find out how this goes.

578
00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:33.350
Guess what?

579
00:28:33.790 --> 00:28:34.430
Not well.

580
00:28:34.790 --> 00:28:36.610
Should, maybe, but shornt.

581
00:28:37.190 --> 00:28:37.750
That's right.

582
00:28:38.050 --> 00:28:49.920
Well, we're back.

583
00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:51.340
We're in Michael's office.

584
00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:54.800
Andy and Kevin are really making a passionate plea to Michael

585
00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:57.800
to please do something about these parking spots

586
00:28:57.800 --> 00:28:59.500
that the construction guys are blocking.

587
00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:01.300
Michael says, you know what?

588
00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:05.040
He's got an assigned parking spot, so not really affecting him.

589
00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:08.900
And there is a couplet of dialogue, Jenna, that just cracked me up.

590
00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:10.300
Seven minutes, four seconds.

591
00:29:10.940 --> 00:29:12.960
Michael says, I wish I could, but I can't.

592
00:29:13.060 --> 00:29:14.100
Well, can, but won't.

593
00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:15.200
Should, maybe, shornt.

594
00:29:15.780 --> 00:29:17.140
And Kevin's like, Michael, please.

595
00:29:17.500 --> 00:29:22.340
And then Michael says, what part of shornt don't you understand, Kevin?

596
00:29:24.290 --> 00:29:26.710
What part of shornt don't you understand?

597
00:29:27.510 --> 00:29:28.550
They're on their own.

598
00:29:28.770 --> 00:29:29.770
He's not going to help them.

599
00:29:30.390 --> 00:29:34.150
No, but he does give them permission to fix the problem themselves.

600
00:29:34.930 --> 00:29:35.190
Yeah.

601
00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:35.710
Right?

602
00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:36.230
Mm-hmm.

603
00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:39.990
He thinks this will be a good thing for them to figure this out.

604
00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:40.430
Right.

605
00:29:40.530 --> 00:29:41.210
An exercise.

606
00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:47.130
Meanwhile, in the break room, I don't even know what title to give this,

607
00:29:47.410 --> 00:29:52.090
everyone is sitting, trying to come up with a name to put on their card for Michael.

608
00:29:52.330 --> 00:29:57.030
We had a fan question from Stephanie, Emily, Laura, and Anne.

609
00:29:57.270 --> 00:30:00.330
When giving Michael names of single women he should ask out,

610
00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:02.570
Jim writes down Pam's mom.

611
00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:03.790
I know.

612
00:30:04.610 --> 00:30:04.950
Right?

613
00:30:05.290 --> 00:30:09.730
Was this an intentional foreshadowing or just a coincidence?

614
00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:12.350
Well, guys, spoiler alert.

615
00:30:12.690 --> 00:30:17.510
In future seasons, Michael is going to date Pam's mom.

616
00:30:17.750 --> 00:30:20.370
It was a very controversial storyline.

617
00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:21.970
We'll get to it.

618
00:30:22.230 --> 00:30:28.770
I think this was the first time the writers had ever even thought of this idea.

619
00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:35.470
It was just a funny joke in the moment, but I have to believe that it stayed in their brains.

620
00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:37.190
It planted a seed.

621
00:30:37.590 --> 00:30:43.470
Well, there's a deleted scene between Michael and Kelly where Michael's like, Kelly, who are you going to set me up with?

622
00:30:43.930 --> 00:30:46.590
And she's like, well, I have a friend who's 23.

623
00:30:47.270 --> 00:30:48.230
And he's like, yeah.

624
00:30:48.350 --> 00:30:52.730
And she's like, oh, you know, I actually have the perfect group to set you up with.

625
00:30:53.110 --> 00:30:55.350
My friend's parents who've gotten divorced.

626
00:30:55.350 --> 00:30:58.910
And Michael's like, oh, and he walks off disgusted.

627
00:30:58.910 --> 00:31:08.650
So there is sort of this running joke in this episode that didn't fully make it in that, like, Kelly is going to set Michael up with this whole older group of people.

628
00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:11.090
The divorcee.

629
00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:17.250
I guess I'll say the writers at this time didn't know yet that they were going to write that storyline.

630
00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:19.410
But I think this was the beginning of something.

631
00:31:20.030 --> 00:31:21.810
It's the group mind, lady.

632
00:31:22.050 --> 00:31:23.670
There it is in action.

633
00:31:24.170 --> 00:31:27.150
I love that Michael calls Wendy.

634
00:31:29.290 --> 00:31:31.070
It's so funny.

635
00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:33.310
Do you know who the voice of Wendy was?

636
00:31:33.310 --> 00:31:44.850
We got a lot of mail about it, and I wrote to Kent Zabornak, and he did not remember who we cast, but one of the finalists for the role was Lindsay Broad,

637
00:31:44.870 --> 00:31:48.450
who ends up playing an arc on the show.

638
00:31:48.810 --> 00:31:49.130
Yeah.

639
00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:51.750
Now, I don't think she got the role.

640
00:31:51.950 --> 00:31:55.210
I really listened to the voice, and I think it was someone else.

641
00:31:55.650 --> 00:31:57.610
So I don't have the answer.

642
00:31:57.610 --> 00:32:02.970
But I thought it was very interesting that Lindsay had auditioned for just this voice role.

643
00:32:03.650 --> 00:32:06.510
Well, you know who's on the case.

644
00:32:06.770 --> 00:32:08.230
Who's always on the case?

645
00:32:08.510 --> 00:32:09.010
Dwight.

646
00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:09.810
Dwight.

647
00:32:09.970 --> 00:32:10.570
He's on the case.

648
00:32:10.730 --> 00:32:13.690
He's going to find Michael, the chair model.

649
00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:14.270
Yeah.

650
00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:21.590
And he says, I will find her, and I will bring her to you, and as God is my witness, she shall bear your fruit.

651
00:32:21.590 --> 00:32:31.610
This is such good writing, in my opinion, that we set up that Dwight is going to go on this quest, and the quest literally lasts one scene.

652
00:32:32.630 --> 00:32:43.430
Like, I think the expectation in writing is that we're now going to watch Dwight go on a search for the chair model, but then he sums it all up in one talking head.

653
00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:45.670
Yeah, he basically only had to make two phone calls.

654
00:32:45.670 --> 00:32:54.450
So the furniture company put him in touch with the advertising agency, who put him in touch with the photographer, who put him in touch with the modeling agency,

655
00:32:54.450 --> 00:32:59.550
and the modeling agency told him that she died in a car accident.

656
00:32:59.830 --> 00:33:00.870
Case closed.

657
00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:01.730
Case closed.

658
00:33:02.230 --> 00:33:03.010
Investigation over.

659
00:33:03.150 --> 00:33:09.770
And by the way, he's super proud of his investigation, and it's not really landing on him that this is horrible news.

660
00:33:10.250 --> 00:33:12.010
Michael takes the news really hard.

661
00:33:12.210 --> 00:33:14.610
He has to sit down in Dwight's chair.

662
00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:15.390
Yeah.

663
00:33:15.930 --> 00:33:18.670
And Jim says, Michael, you didn't even know her.

664
00:33:20.730 --> 00:33:21.170
Gasp.

665
00:33:21.530 --> 00:33:23.530
Even Pam shakes her head at Jim.

666
00:33:23.530 --> 00:33:23.770
I know.

667
00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:25.210
Like, Jim, not now.

668
00:33:25.630 --> 00:33:27.830
Michael's like, try not to be so hurtful, Jim.

669
00:33:27.990 --> 00:33:29.330
Dwight's like, how dare you?

670
00:33:29.730 --> 00:33:33.710
And one of my favorite lines of this whole episode is coming up.

671
00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:34.790
What is it?

672
00:33:35.070 --> 00:33:37.170
Pam says, okay, Michael, you know what?

673
00:33:37.310 --> 00:33:39.110
I might have someone for you.

674
00:33:39.270 --> 00:33:40.890
And Michael says, oh, really?

675
00:33:41.050 --> 00:33:41.890
What's her name?

676
00:33:42.050 --> 00:33:42.790
Burger King.

677
00:33:43.250 --> 00:33:45.210
Oh, yeah, that made me laugh out loud, too.

678
00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:48.110
Oh, it made me laugh so hard.

679
00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:56.850
Pam, for whatever reason, decides to give Michael the phone number of her landlady.

680
00:33:57.470 --> 00:33:57.950
Margaret.

681
00:33:58.610 --> 00:33:59.090
Mm-hmm.

682
00:33:59.350 --> 00:34:03.470
She says she's really nice, and I think you might get along.

683
00:34:04.210 --> 00:34:04.450
Oh.

684
00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:06.670
I mean, Michael's jazzed.

685
00:34:06.690 --> 00:34:07.270
He's excited.

686
00:34:07.490 --> 00:34:10.070
He starts brushing his teeth in the kitchen sink.

687
00:34:10.250 --> 00:34:12.110
Why can't he do that in the men's room?

688
00:34:12.270 --> 00:34:13.150
Well, don't worry, Angela.

689
00:34:13.290 --> 00:34:14.550
He's not going to spit it out.

690
00:34:14.690 --> 00:34:15.790
He swallows it.

691
00:34:15.910 --> 00:34:16.830
Did you notice that?

692
00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:18.730
Yes, so disgusting.

693
00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:20.850
Also, electric toothbrush.

694
00:34:21.210 --> 00:34:26.870
Also, remember when we were wondering in local ad, does he have a toothbrush in the office?

695
00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:28.030
He does.

696
00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:31.350
So now, the five families are going to be assembled.

697
00:34:31.950 --> 00:34:32.950
Andy and Kevin are doing it.

698
00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:37.830
Kevin has a talking head where he breaks down the five families, how they rarely ever meet.

699
00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:43.470
Yeah, and they have this great footage of each member of the five families.

700
00:34:43.470 --> 00:34:44.350
It's pretty amazing.

701
00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:47.850
I have two little fun facts from this.

702
00:34:48.070 --> 00:34:48.270
Okay.

703
00:34:48.450 --> 00:34:50.990
So now, when they show W.B.

704
00:34:51.110 --> 00:34:56.590
Jones, they kind of show a clip of what looks like a commercial that he's in,

705
00:34:56.710 --> 00:34:59.150
where he's selling his air conditioners, right?

706
00:34:59.350 --> 00:34:59.530
Right.

707
00:34:59.710 --> 00:35:03.550
There's a 1-800 number in that commercial on the screen.

708
00:35:03.550 --> 00:35:04.590
Did you call it?

709
00:35:04.590 --> 00:35:10.010
I didn't need to call it because it's the exact same number that was on the flyer in

710
00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:13.510
Women's Appreciation, which we know is a sex hotline.

711
00:35:13.690 --> 00:35:16.090
Why do we keep recycling this number?

712
00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:21.010
I think we must have bought this number because after this episode came out,

713
00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:23.690
they put an outgoing message for W.B.

714
00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:24.150
Jones.

715
00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:26.390
So if you called it, you got that message.

716
00:35:26.870 --> 00:35:28.610
Yeah, we must have owned it for a bit.

717
00:35:28.710 --> 00:35:30.530
And we clearly don't own it anymore.

718
00:35:30.730 --> 00:35:31.830
We really don't.

719
00:35:31.830 --> 00:35:37.650
Well, the other fun fact I have about this little montage is that at 10 minutes, 55 seconds,

720
00:35:37.910 --> 00:35:42.030
we see a photo of cool guy Paul sitting on a motorcycle.

721
00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:45.270
And there's a very interesting story here.

722
00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:47.830
Sam, will you play this clip from Paul?

723
00:35:48.910 --> 00:35:52.830
Hey, so a quick story about that motorcycle picture, because a lot of people ask me,

724
00:35:52.890 --> 00:35:53.690
is that your motorcycle?

725
00:35:53.690 --> 00:36:00.010
And no, it's actually we were shooting the scenes and we were supposed to shoot somewhere

726
00:36:00.010 --> 00:36:04.650
on some guy's house in the back deck overlooking the ocean, but it got late in the day

727
00:36:04.650 --> 00:36:09.490
and they didn't want to move the whole crew and the cameras and the lighting and all that stuff.

728
00:36:09.710 --> 00:36:16.070
So right behind the lot where they shoot all the scenes where the office is, is a residential neighborhood.

729
00:36:16.070 --> 00:36:21.350
So we went through the fence and it was some guy's house and we just set up on the front lawn.

730
00:36:21.470 --> 00:36:25.090
I believe it was the motorcycle of one of the camera guys.

731
00:36:25.190 --> 00:36:26.450
He happens to ride motorcycles.

732
00:36:26.790 --> 00:36:28.290
So he brought his motorcycle around.

733
00:36:28.510 --> 00:36:29.430
I sat on it.

734
00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:33.930
We took a bunch of pictures and that just became the shot associated with cool guy Paul.

735
00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:38.870
And it saved having to move everybody to another location and spend hours doing it.

736
00:36:39.010 --> 00:36:41.390
So people always ask me, are you a motorcycle?

737
00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:42.370
How'd you get the motorcycle?

738
00:36:42.370 --> 00:36:46.950
But nope, it was just a quick shot of one of the crew members' motorcycles.

739
00:36:48.070 --> 00:36:53.550
Well, Jenna, I reached out to Kenneth Paul, who did my makeup on the show, because he rode a Harley, right?

740
00:36:53.870 --> 00:36:54.350
To set.

741
00:36:54.610 --> 00:36:55.890
Every day to work.

742
00:36:56.050 --> 00:36:59.430
